============================================================
              CHAPTER 12 — POINTERS IN C
       Complete Deep-Dive | Job-Ready | Interview Ready
============================================================

Pointers are one of the MOST IMPORTANT topics in C.

They are heavily used in:
    • Dynamic memory allocation
    • Arrays and strings
    • Functions
    • Data structures
    • Operating systems
    • Embedded systems
    • System programming
    • File handling
    • Linked lists, trees, graphs
    • Low-level programming

------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT IS A POINTER?
------------------------------------------------------------

A pointer is a variable that stores the address of another object.

Example:

    int x = 10;
    int *p = &x;

Memory concept:

    x
    +------+
    |  10  |
    +------+
      ^
      |
      |
    p stores the address of x

Here:

    x       -> integer object
    &x      -> address of x
    p       -> pointer containing address of x
    *p      -> value stored at the address in p

Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int x = 10;
        int *p = &x;

        printf("x  = %d\n", x);
        printf("&x = %p\n", (void *)&x);
        printf("p  = %p\n", (void *)p);
        printf("*p = %d\n", *p);

        return 0;
    }

Possible output:

    x  = 10
    &x = 0x...
    p  = 0x...
    *p = 10

The exact address is different every time/program/system.

------------------------------------------------------------
2. WHY DO WE NEED POINTERS?
------------------------------------------------------------

Pointers allow a program to work with memory addresses.

Major uses:

1. Modify variables inside functions
2. Work efficiently with arrays
3. Process strings
4. Dynamic memory allocation
5. Create linked lists
6. Create trees and graphs
7. Function pointers/callbacks
8. System programming
9. Hardware/embedded programming
10. Avoid unnecessary copying

Example:

    int x = 10;
    int *p = &x;

    *p = 50;

Now:

    x == 50

Because *p accesses x itself.

------------------------------------------------------------
3. MEMORY ADDRESS
------------------------------------------------------------

Every object stored in memory has an address.

Example:

    int x = 100;

Conceptually:

    Address        Value
    ---------------------
    0x1000          100

If:

    int *p = &x;

then:

    p = 0x1000

and:

    *p = 100

IMPORTANT:

Never assume a real memory address such as 0x1000.
The actual address depends on the system and execution.

------------------------------------------------------------
4. ADDRESS-OF OPERATOR &
------------------------------------------------------------

The & operator obtains the address of an object.

Example:

    int x = 25;

    printf("%p\n", (void *)&x);

Correct format for an object pointer:

    %p

and cast to:

    (void *)

Example:

    int x = 25;
    int *p = &x;

Here:

    &x -> address of x

------------------------------------------------------------
5. DEREFERENCE OPERATOR *
------------------------------------------------------------

The * operator can dereference a pointer.

Example:

    int x = 25;
    int *p = &x;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

Output:

    25

You can also modify x:

    *p = 100;

Now:

    x == 100

Important distinction:

    int *p;     // declaration: p is pointer to int

    *p          // expression: dereference p

    &x          // address of x

------------------------------------------------------------
6. POINTER DECLARATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

    data_type *pointer_name;

Examples:

    int *p;
    char *c;
    float *f;
    double *d;

Meaning:

    int *p
        p points to int

    char *c
        c points to char

    float *f
        f points to float

    double *d
        d points to double

The pointer's type tells C what kind of object the pointer
is intended to point to and affects operations such as
dereferencing and pointer arithmetic.

------------------------------------------------------------
7. INITIALIZING A POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

Never use an uninitialized pointer.

BAD:

    int *p;
    *p = 10;       // Undefined behavior

Why?

Because p contains an indeterminate value.

GOOD:

    int x = 10;
    int *p = &x;

Or:

    int *p = NULL;

Then assign a valid address later.

------------------------------------------------------------
8. NULL POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

A NULL pointer represents that the pointer does not currently
point to a valid object.

Example:

    int *p = NULL;

You must NOT dereference it:

    *p = 10;       // Undefined behavior

Always check when appropriate:

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        printf("%d\n", *p);
    }

NULL is a null pointer constant/macro, while '\0' is the
null character used to terminate C strings.

Do not confuse:

    NULL
    '\0'
    0

They have different roles.

------------------------------------------------------------
9. POINTER TYPES
------------------------------------------------------------

Different pointer types can point to different object types.

Example:

    int x = 10;
    char c = 'A';
    double d = 3.14;

    int *p1 = &x;
    char *p2 = &c;
    double *p3 = &d;

Conceptually:

    p1 -> int
    p2 -> char
    p3 -> double

Pointer arithmetic depends on the pointed-to type.

------------------------------------------------------------
10. POINTER SIZE
------------------------------------------------------------

On many modern 64-bit systems, object pointers are commonly
8 bytes, but this is NOT guaranteed by the C language.

Example:

    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(int *));
    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(char *));
    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(double *));

Do not assume all pointer sizes universally.

Use:

    sizeof(pointer)

to determine the size on the current implementation.

------------------------------------------------------------
11. POINTER AND DATA TYPE
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int x = 100;
    int *p = &x;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

The compiler knows that p points to an int.

This matters for:

    *p
    p + 1
    p - 1

------------------------------------------------------------
12. POINTER ARITHMETIC
------------------------------------------------------------

Pointer arithmetic is especially useful with arrays.

Example:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

    int *p = arr;

Then:

    *p       -> 10
    *(p + 1) -> 20
    *(p + 2) -> 30
    *(p + 3) -> 40

The pointer increment moves by the size of the pointed-to
object, not simply one byte.

If:

    sizeof(int) == 4

then conceptually:

    p       -> address A
    p + 1   -> A + 4 bytes
    p + 2   -> A + 8 bytes

But do not manually assume sizeof(int) is always 4.

------------------------------------------------------------
13. POINTER INCREMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};

    int *p = arr;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

    p++;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

Output:

    10
    20

Again:

    p++

moves to the next int object.

------------------------------------------------------------
14. POINTER DECREMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};

    int *p = &arr[2];

    printf("%d\n", *p);

    p--;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

Output:

    30
    20

------------------------------------------------------------
15. POINTER + INTEGER
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

    int *p = arr;

    printf("%d\n", *(p + 2));

Output:

    30

Equivalent:

    arr[2]

because:

    arr[i] == *(arr + i)

------------------------------------------------------------
16. POINTER - INTEGER
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

    int *p = &arr[3];

    printf("%d\n", *(p - 2));

Output:

    20

------------------------------------------------------------
17. POINTER - POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

Two pointers into the same array can be subtracted.

Example:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

    int *p = &arr[4];
    int *q = &arr[1];

    printf("%td\n", p - q);

Output:

    3

Because there are three array-element positions between them.

Pointer subtraction is meaningful when the pointers point into
the same array object (or one past it).

The result type is:

    ptrdiff_t

from:

    <stddef.h>

------------------------------------------------------------
18. POINTER COMPARISON
------------------------------------------------------------

Pointers can be compared in certain meaningful contexts.

For pointers into the same array:

    p < q
    p > q
    p <= q
    p >= q

can be used to compare their positions.

Equality comparisons:

    p == q
    p != q

can be used to determine whether two pointers represent the
same address.

Do not treat arbitrary unrelated object pointers as if they
were positions in one common array for relational comparison.

------------------------------------------------------------
19. POINTERS AND ARRAYS
------------------------------------------------------------

One of the most important relationships in C:

For most expressions, an array expression is converted to a
pointer to its first element.

Example:

    int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

    int *p = arr;

This is equivalent to:

    int *p = &arr[0];

Therefore:

    arr[0]
    *(arr + 0)

are equivalent.

Similarly:

    arr[2]
    *(arr + 2)

are equivalent.

------------------------------------------------------------
20. ARRAY TRAVERSAL USING POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        size_t n = sizeof arr / sizeof arr[0];

        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            printf("%d ", *(arr + i));
        }

        return 0;
    }

Output:

    10 20 30 40 50

------------------------------------------------------------
21. POINTER WALKING
------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of using an index:

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);

you can use a pointer:

    int *p = arr;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", *p);
        p++;
    }

Another common form:

    int *end = arr + n;

    for (int *p = arr; p < end; p++)
    {
        printf("%d ", *p);
    }

This is useful in low-level C programming.

------------------------------------------------------------
22. ARRAY-TO-POINTER CONVERSION
------------------------------------------------------------

Given:

    int arr[5];

In most expressions:

    arr

becomes:

    &arr[0]

But there are important exceptions.

For example:

    sizeof(arr)

does NOT convert arr to a pointer.

Also:

    &arr

gives the address of the entire array.

This difference is extremely important.

------------------------------------------------------------
23. arr vs &arr
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[5];

Then:

    arr
        type in most expressions:
        pointer to first element, int *

    &arr
        type:
        pointer to entire array, int (*)[5]

They may have the same numeric address, but their types differ.

Example:

    printf("%p\n", (void *)arr);
    printf("%p\n", (void *)&arr);

Addresses are commonly numerically equal, but the pointer
types and pointer arithmetic are different.

------------------------------------------------------------
24. POINTER TO POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

A pointer can point to another pointer.

Example:

    int x = 10;

    int *p = &x;

    int **pp = &p;

Conceptually:

    x
    ^
    |
    p
    ^
    |
    pp

Access:

    x      -> 10
    *p     -> 10
    **pp   -> 10

Example:

    **pp = 50;

Now:

    x == 50

------------------------------------------------------------
25. MULTIPLE INDIRECTION
------------------------------------------------------------

You can have:

    int *p;
    int **pp;
    int ***ppp;

Example:

    int x = 10;

    int *p = &x;
    int **pp = &p;
    int ***ppp = &pp;

Then:

    *p       -> x
    **pp     -> x
    ***ppp   -> x

Multiple pointers are used in advanced data structures and
functions that need to modify pointer variables.

------------------------------------------------------------
26. POINTERS AND FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

C passes function arguments by value.

Pointers allow a function to modify an object owned by the
caller.

Example:

    void change(int *p)
    {
        *p = 100;
    }

    int main(void)
    {
        int x = 10;

        change(&x);

        printf("%d\n", x);

        return 0;
    }

Output:

    100

Important:

C does NOT have pass-by-reference in the C++ sense.

Instead:

    address is passed by value

and the function uses that address to access the caller's
object.

------------------------------------------------------------
27. SWAP TWO VARIABLES USING POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    void swap(int *a, int *b)
    {
        int temp = *a;
        *a = *b;
        *b = temp;
    }

Usage:

    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;

    swap(&x, &y);

After:

    x = 20
    y = 10

This is a classic interview question.

------------------------------------------------------------
28. PASSING ARRAYS TO FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    void printArray(const int arr[], size_t n)
    {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            printf("%d ", arr[i]);
        }
    }

Call:

    int a[] = {10, 20, 30};

    printArray(a, 3);

Inside a function parameter list:

    int arr[]

is adjusted to:

    int *arr

Therefore the function does NOT automatically know the array
length.

You normally pass the length separately.

------------------------------------------------------------
29. const WITH POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

There are three important forms.

A) Pointer to const:

    const int *p;

B) Const pointer:

    int *const p = &x;

C) Const pointer to const:

    const int *const p = &x;

Understand these carefully.

------------------------------------------------------------
30. POINTER TO CONST
------------------------------------------------------------

    const int *p;

Means:

    You can change where p points,
    but you cannot modify the int through p.

Example:

    int a = 10;
    int b = 20;

    const int *p = &a;

    p = &b;       // OK

    *p = 30;      // ERROR

But:

    b = 30;       // OK

The const applies to the pointed-to int through this pointer.

------------------------------------------------------------
31. CONST POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

    int *const p = &x;

Means:

    p cannot point somewhere else,
    but the object can be modified through p.

Example:

    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;

    int *const p = &x;

    *p = 50;      // OK

    p = &y;       // ERROR

------------------------------------------------------------
32. CONST POINTER TO CONST
------------------------------------------------------------

    const int *const p = &x;

Means:

    p cannot change its target

AND:

    *p cannot modify the target

Example:

    int x = 10;

    const int *const p = &x;

    *p = 20;      // ERROR
    p = NULL;     // ERROR

------------------------------------------------------------
33. VOID POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

A void pointer:

    void *

can hold the address of an object of any object type.

Example:

    int x = 10;

    void *p = &x;

But you cannot directly dereference a void pointer in standard C
because its pointed-to type is unknown.

You can convert it to an appropriate pointer type:

    printf("%d\n", *(int *)p);

void * is heavily used by generic library functions such as:

    malloc()
    qsort()
    bsearch()

------------------------------------------------------------
34. POINTER CASTING
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int x = 10;

    void *vp = &x;

    int *ip = vp;

In C, conversion from void * to an object pointer type is
implicit.

Explicit cast:

    int *ip = (int *)vp;

For normal C code, unnecessary casts of malloc's return value
are generally discouraged:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

not:

    int *p = (int *)malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

------------------------------------------------------------
35. POINTERS AND STRINGS
------------------------------------------------------------

Strings are character arrays.

Example:

    char str[] = "Hello";

You can use:

    char *p = str;

Then:

    *p       -> 'H'
    *(p + 1) -> 'e'
    *(p + 2) -> 'l'

Example:

    while (*p != '\0')
    {
        putchar(*p);
        p++;
    }

Output:

    Hello

------------------------------------------------------------
36. char ARRAY VS char POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

Important difference:

    char str[] = "Hello";

This creates a modifiable array initialized with the characters
of the string literal.

But:

    const char *p = "Hello";

p points to a string literal. The program must not modify that
literal.

Therefore:

    str[0] = 'Y';      // OK

while:

    p[0] = 'Y';       // Undefined behavior

Use:

    const char *p

when pointing to a string literal.

------------------------------------------------------------
37. POINTER TO STRUCTURE
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    struct Student
    {
        int age;
        float marks;
    };

    struct Student s = {22, 90.5f};

    struct Student *p = &s;

Access:

    (*p).age

or more conveniently:

    p->age

Similarly:

    p->marks

------------------------------------------------------------
38. . VS ->
------------------------------------------------------------

For an actual structure object:

    s.age

For a pointer to structure:

    p->age

This:

    p->age

is equivalent to:

    (*p).age

The parentheses are necessary in the expanded form.

------------------------------------------------------------
39. DYNAMIC MEMORY AND POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Pointers are essential for dynamic memory.

Main functions:

    malloc()
    calloc()
    realloc()
    free()

Header:

    #include <stdlib.h>

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

If allocation succeeds:

    p[0]
    p[1]
    p[2]
    p[3]
    p[4]

can be used.

Always check allocation when failure matters:

    if (p == NULL)
    {
        /* handle allocation failure */
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
40. malloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

    malloc(number_of_bytes)

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

malloc allocates uninitialized storage.

The allocated bytes have indeterminate values for ordinary
object use until appropriately initialized.

Initialize them yourself.

Example:

    for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        p[i] = 0;
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
41. calloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

    calloc(number_of_elements, element_size)

Example:

    int *p = calloc(5, sizeof *p);

The allocated storage is initialized to all-bits-zero.

For integer types, this commonly corresponds to zero, but do not
generalize "all-bits-zero" as a universal representation for
every possible C type.

------------------------------------------------------------
42. realloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

realloc changes the size of a previously allocated block.

Example:

    int *temp = realloc(p, 10 * sizeof *p);

Good practice:

    int *temp = realloc(p, 10 * sizeof *p);

    if (temp != NULL)
    {
        p = temp;
    }
    else
    {
        /* p is still valid */
    }

Do NOT immediately overwrite p like:

    p = realloc(p, new_size);

if you need to preserve p on allocation failure.

------------------------------------------------------------
43. free()
------------------------------------------------------------

Dynamic memory must eventually be released.

Example:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        /* use p */

        free(p);
        p = NULL;
    }

Setting the pointer to NULL after free can help prevent accidental
reuse of that pointer variable.

------------------------------------------------------------
44. DANGLING POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

A dangling pointer points to an object whose lifetime has ended.

Example:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    free(p);

Now p still contains the old address, but that storage is no
longer owned by the program.

Therefore:

    *p = 10;       // Undefined behavior

Safer:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
45. WILD POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

A wild pointer is an uninitialized pointer.

Example:

    int *p;

    *p = 100;      // Undefined behavior

Correct:

    int x;
    int *p = &x;

or:

    int *p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
46. MEMORY LEAK
------------------------------------------------------------

A memory leak occurs when dynamically allocated memory becomes
unreachable without being freed.

Example:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

    p = NULL;

The allocated block can no longer be reached.

That memory is leaked until the process ends.

Correct:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
47. DOUBLE FREE
------------------------------------------------------------

Calling free twice on the same allocated object is invalid.

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    free(p);
    free(p);       // Undefined behavior

Safer pattern:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

Then:

    free(p);       // free(NULL) is safe

But do not rely on this to hide incorrect ownership design.

------------------------------------------------------------
48. USE-AFTER-FREE
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    *p = 10;

    free(p);

    printf("%d\n", *p);     // Undefined behavior

The object lifetime has ended.

------------------------------------------------------------
49. POINTER ALIASING
------------------------------------------------------------

Two pointers may point to the same object.

Example:

    int x = 10;

    int *p = &x;
    int *q = &x;

Then:

    *p = 50;

Therefore:

    *q == 50

because both point to x.

Aliasing is powerful but can make programs harder to reason
about.

------------------------------------------------------------
50. RESTRICT
------------------------------------------------------------

C provides the restrict qualifier.

Example:

    void add(size_t n,
             int *restrict dst,
             const int *restrict src)
    {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
            dst[i] += src[i];
    }

restrict tells the compiler about intended access relationships
under the rules of the C language and can enable optimization.

Using restrict incorrectly can result in undefined behavior.

It is an advanced topic and should be learned after basic pointers
and aliasing.

------------------------------------------------------------
51. POINTER TO ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[3][4];

    int (*p)[4] = arr;

Here:

    p

is a pointer to an array of 4 int.

Then:

    p + 1

moves to the next row.

This is different from:

    int *p;

------------------------------------------------------------
52. ARRAY OF POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    const char *names[] =
    {
        "Apoorv",
        "Rahul",
        "Amit"
    };

Here:

    names

is an array whose elements are pointers to char
(and the pointed-to string literals should not be modified).

Conceptually:

    names[0] -> "Apoorv"
    names[1] -> "Rahul"
    names[2] -> "Amit"

------------------------------------------------------------
53. POINTER TO POINTER WITH FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

A pointer-to-pointer is useful when a function must change the
caller’s pointer.

Example:

    void allocate_int(int **pp)
    {
        *pp = malloc(sizeof **pp);

        if (*pp != NULL)
        {
            **pp = 100;
        }
    }

Usage:

    int *p = NULL;

    allocate_int(&p);

After successful allocation:

    p != NULL
    *p == 100

This technique is common in linked-list operations.

------------------------------------------------------------
54. LINKED LIST CONNECTION
------------------------------------------------------------

A linked-list node commonly contains a pointer to another node.

Example:

    struct Node
    {
        int data;
        struct Node *next;
    };

Conceptually:

    [10 | next] -> [20 | next] -> [30 | NULL]

This is one of the biggest practical reasons to master pointers.

------------------------------------------------------------
55. FUNCTION POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

A function pointer stores the address of a function.

Example:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }

    int (*fp)(int, int) = add;

Then:

    printf("%d\n", fp(10, 20));

Output:

    30

Equivalent call:

    (*fp)(10, 20)

------------------------------------------------------------
56. CALLBACK FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

A callback is a function passed to another function through a
function pointer.

Example:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }

    int multiply(int a, int b)
    {
        return a * b;
    }

    int calculate(int a, int b,
                  int (*operation)(int, int))
    {
        return operation(a, b);
    }

Usage:

    printf("%d\n", calculate(5, 3, add));
    printf("%d\n", calculate(5, 3, multiply));

Output:

    8
    15

Callbacks are heavily used in:

    qsort()
    event systems
    GUI programming
    operating systems
    libraries
    asynchronous programming

------------------------------------------------------------
57. COMPLEX POINTER DECLARATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p;

Read:

    p is a pointer to int.

Example:

    int **p;

Read:

    p is a pointer to pointer to int.

Example:

    int (*p)[5];

Read:

    p is a pointer to an array of 5 int.

Example:

    int *p[5];

Read:

    p is an array of 5 pointers to int.

These two are VERY different:

    int (*p)[5];

    int *p[5];

Remember:

    [] and () bind more tightly than *

------------------------------------------------------------
58. POINTER CASTING AND TYPE SAFETY
------------------------------------------------------------

Avoid unnecessary pointer casts.

Bad style:

    int *p = (int *)some_pointer;

unless the conversion is actually needed and correct.

Wrong pointer interpretation can cause:

    • Misalignment
    • Incorrect object representation
    • Strict aliasing violations
    • Undefined behavior

Pointers are not simply "integers containing addresses."

------------------------------------------------------------
59. ALIGNMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

Objects may require particular memory alignment.

For example:

    int

may have stricter alignment requirements than:

    char

Do not create arbitrary typed pointers by taking an arbitrary byte
address and assuming it is correctly aligned.

Incorrect alignment can cause undefined behavior on implementations
where the required alignment is not satisfied.

------------------------------------------------------------
60. EFFECTIVE TYPE / ALIASING BASICS
------------------------------------------------------------

C has rules about how an object's stored value may be accessed
through different types.

Do not assume this is always safe:

    int x = 10;

    float *p = (float *)&x;

    printf("%f\n", *p);    // problematic/undefined behavior

The fact that two types occupy similar numbers of bytes does not
make such type-punning automatically valid.

For raw byte inspection/copying, character types have special
status under C's object representation rules.

------------------------------------------------------------
61. memcpy AND POINTERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int source[] = {1, 2, 3};
    int destination[3];

    memcpy(destination,
           source,
           sizeof source);

Header:

    #include <string.h>

For overlapping regions:

    memcpy()      -> not valid for overlap
    memmove()     -> designed for overlap

------------------------------------------------------------
62. POINTER AND sizeof
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int arr[10];

    sizeof(arr)

returns the total size of the array.

But:

    int *p = arr;

    sizeof(p)

returns the size of the pointer.

Therefore:

    sizeof(arr) != sizeof(p)

in general.

This is a common interview question.

------------------------------------------------------------
63. ARRAY LENGTH VS POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

This works:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

    size_t n = sizeof arr / sizeof arr[0];

But this does NOT work inside a function parameter after
array-to-pointer adjustment:

    void test(int arr[])
    {
        sizeof(arr)       // size of pointer, not entire array
    }

Therefore pass the size:

    void test(const int arr[], size_t n)

------------------------------------------------------------
64. ONE-PAST-THE-END POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

For:

    int arr[5];

This is valid:

    int *end = arr + 5;

But:

    *end

is NOT valid because it does not point to an element.

You can use it as a boundary:

    for (int *p = arr; p < end; p++)
    {
        printf("%d ", *p);
    }

The one-past pointer may be formed and compared/subtracted
appropriately, but must not be dereferenced.

------------------------------------------------------------
65. POINTER AND NULL TERMINATED STRING
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    char str[] = "Hello";

Memory:

    H
    e
    l
    l
    o
    \0

Pointer traversal:

    char *p = str;

    while (*p != '\0')
    {
        putchar(*p);
        p++;
    }

This is the fundamental mechanism behind many string functions.

------------------------------------------------------------
66. COMMON POINTER BUG #1
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p;

    printf("%d", *p);

Problem:

    p is uninitialized.

Fix:

    int x = 10;
    int *p = &x;

------------------------------------------------------------
67. COMMON POINTER BUG #2
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = NULL;

    *p = 10;

Problem:

    dereferencing NULL.

------------------------------------------------------------
68. COMMON POINTER BUG #3
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    free(p);

    *p = 10;

Problem:

    use-after-free.

------------------------------------------------------------
69. COMMON POINTER BUG #4
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

    p = NULL;

Problem:

    memory leak.

Correct:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
70. COMMON POINTER BUG #5
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

    free(p);
    free(p);

Problem:

    double free / undefined behavior.

------------------------------------------------------------
71. COMMON POINTER BUG #6
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *getValue(void)
    {
        int x = 10;
        return &x;
    }

The lifetime of x ends when the function returns.

Returning its address creates an invalid/dangling pointer.

------------------------------------------------------------
72. STATIC STORAGE EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Returning a pointer to static storage can be valid:

    int *getValue(void)
    {
        static int x = 10;
        return &x;
    }

Here x has static storage duration.

However, this introduces shared state and is not automatically
the best design.

------------------------------------------------------------
73. POINTER OWNERSHIP
------------------------------------------------------------

In larger programs, always ask:

    Who allocated this memory?
    Who owns it?
    Who is responsible for freeing it?
    How long is it valid?
    Can another pointer alias it?
    Can it become NULL?
    Can the object be modified?

A large percentage of real-world C memory bugs are ownership
and lifetime bugs.

------------------------------------------------------------
74. POINTER LIFETIME
------------------------------------------------------------

A pointer itself has a lifetime.

The object it points to has its own lifetime.

These are not necessarily the same.

Example:

    int *p;

    {
        int x = 10;
        p = &x;
    }

After the block ends:

    x no longer exists

Therefore:

    p

becomes a dangling pointer.

------------------------------------------------------------
75. STACK VS HEAP EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Automatic/local:

    void test(void)
    {
        int x = 10;
        int *p = &x;
    }

x normally has automatic storage duration.

Dynamic:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

The allocated object exists until:

    free(p);

The exact physical implementation of "stack" and "heap" is
implementation-dependent, but this distinction is useful for
understanding typical systems.

